# The TLS option refusals of `curl` and `wget` (ADR-0321; `make test-apps`,
# which turns the @TLS...@ ports into numbers, as for apps.script). A run of its
# own, so that apps.script and this one each stay within the runner's timeout
# under TCG: every line is a launch of a 6.7 MiB program. The refusal happens
# before any connection, but each line has the network a transfer would need,
# and the test servers run during this run: the Makefile checks that the
# expired and wrong-name servers saw 0 request bytes.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
# The lease takes a few hundred milliseconds under TCG.
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# Nothing in the programs turns the checks off or changes the roots (ADR-0321,
# INV-0321-1; ADR-0228 item 3, INV-0312-1). The lines below do not match a
# description, so they run with default settings, and the +net word gives the
# network that a transfer would need: the programs themselves refuse each
# option, exit with status 2, and the Makefile checks that the expired and
# wrong-name servers saw 0 request bytes. The roots a --cacert or --capath
# names are readable (+r), the other root of the test PKI.
> +net expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@ curl -k https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< is refused
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 2
< \r\n~>
> +net expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@ curl -ks https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< is refused
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 2
< \r\n~>
# -s hides the refusal's own line, not curl's "is badly used here" after it, and
# the status is 2.
> +net expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@ curl -sk https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< is badly used here
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 2
